# Force a known-good kernel command line regardless of what LK/the vendor
# bootloader injects at handoff. Needed because LK unconditionally passes
# `console=ttyMT0,921600n1 ... printk.disable_uart=1` (the vendor console
# name, not a mainline one) -- see boot.md "First 6.6 Flash" (2026-07-04).
# Without CONFIG_CMDLINE_FORCE, that overrides our own DTS chosen/bootargs
# and mainline never attaches a console to the UART.
#
# CHANGED 2026-07-04: switched from the explicit-address generic form
# (`earlycon=uart8250,mmio32,0x11002000`) to the bare DT-node form. The
# explicit form always selects the generic `early_serial8250_setup` driver
# (8250_early.c), never the MediaTek-specific `early_mtk8250_setup`
# (8250_mtk.c), which is only reachable via OF_EARLYCON_DECLARE matching
# uart0's compatible string ("mediatek,mt6797-uart"/"mediatek,mt6577-uart",
# confirmed in mt6797.dtsi). uart0 is reachable via /aliases serial0 and
# /chosen stdout-path (mt6797-gemini-pda.dts), so bare `earlycon` resolves
# to it and picks up the MTK-specific setup (baud/highspeed-divisor
# handling) instead of assuming plain-16550 register behaviour. Every
# capture so far (cmdline-force, KASLR off, RELOCATABLE off/on) has been
# silent right after el3_exit with the generic form -- see boot.md "Aligned
# kernel_addr retested" and the vendor-baseline entry (2026-07-04) for the
# full diagnostic chain. This is the next untested variable.
# ADDED 2026-07-04 (maxcpus=1): the FIRST successful jump-to-K64 boot
# (logs/2026-07-04-21-scp-node-boot.log) reached Linux 6.6, printed the full
# earlycon log, then HUNG at "smp: Bringing up secondary CPUs ..." on CPU1
# (kernel timestamps stop at [0.016897]; ATF's aee watchdog dumps ~14s later
# and resets the SoC -> reboot loop). Boot single-core to get past the
# SMP/PSCI stall and see how far the kernel proceeds. SMP bring-up is a
# separate problem to fix once we have a stable single-CPU boot (bootability
# first, per CLAUDE.md principle 5).
# ADDED 2026-07-04 (clk_ignore_unused): with maxcpus=1 the kernel booted
# cleanly through all driver init (real ttyS0 console handoff, mtk-wdt took
# over the watchdog) and then went SILENT at exactly
# "clk: Disabling unused clocks" (logs/2026-07-04-23-maxcpus1-boot.log,
# [0.475319]) -- no "Freeing unused kernel memory", no root mount, no panic.
# ~31s later the (un-petted) mtk-wdt fires and resets -> preloader loop.
# Classic incomplete-clock-tree hang: the mainline mt6797 clk driver gates a
# clock the hardware silently depends on because nothing in our DT claims it.
# clk_ignore_unused keeps all clocks on so we can get past this into
# userspace; individual gates can be re-enabled once the clock/DT deps are
# understood.
# ADDED 2026-07-04 (nokaslr): the MSDC0 bring-up hangs silently after probe
# with only an ATF aee_wdt_dump giving a PC/LR pair -- with KASLR's boot-time
# slide active those addresses don't match System.map (looked up as bogus
# omap_dm_timer symbols, a driver not even relevant here). nokaslr keeps
# CONFIG_RANDOMIZE_BASE=y but disables the runtime slide, so a captured PC/LR
# can be symbolicated directly against the vmlinux build. Diagnostic aid only;
# remove once the MSDC hang is root-caused. Left in place now that MSDC is
# deferred (harmless either way) since the msdc0 DT node is disabled.
# ADDED 2026-07-04 (rdinit=/bin/sh): MSDC0/eMMC bring-up is deferred (see
# mt6797-gemini-pda.dts node comment, blockers.md B-7) -- the vendor 2019
# Kali ramdisk's /init script unconditionally does `switch_root` onto
# /dev/mmcblk0p29, which cannot exist without a working eMMC controller, and
# always panics (boot.md "SEVENTH RESULT"). Since eMMC is not required to
# prove kernel bootability (CLAUDE.md principle 5, and the Gemini also has a
# removable SD card as an alternative storage path for later), skip the
# vendor init script entirely and exec a shell directly from the initramfs
# instead, to demonstrate a clean boot to userspace independent of storage.
# REMOVED 2026-07-05 (rdinit=/bin/sh): eMMC now enumerates (mmcblk0, all 33
# partitions -- boot.md FIFTEENTH RESULT, mt2701-mmc compat). Let the vendor
# ramdisk /init run its switch_root onto /dev/mmcblk0p29 to test whether the
# 2019 Kali userspace boots under 6.6 (the remaining half of blockers.md B-7).
# REMOVED 2026-07-06 (clk_ignore_unused): root-caused instead of worked
# around. drivers/tty/serial/8250/8250_mtk.c never enabled the UART's own
# "baud"/infra_uart0 clock (only read its rate), so the late-boot
# clk_disable_unused pass correctly cut power to the only console clock still
# in use, which looked exactly like a hang. Fixed with
# patches/v6.6/serial/0001-serial-8250_mtk-hold-baud-clock-enabled.patch
# (devm_clk_get_enabled() instead of devm_clk_get()), validated on hardware
# build #69 (logs/2026-07-06-69-uart-clk-fix-validation/,
# logs/2026-07-06-70-uart-clk-fix-validation-boot.log) with this flag
# removed -- boot proceeds cleanly to systemd. See boot.md "BUILD
# #62/#65/#67/#69" and blockers.md B-2 item 5 (resolved).
# CHANGED 2026-07-06 (maxcpus=1 -> maxcpus=8): PSCI CPU_ON instrumentation
# (arch/arm64/kernel/psci.c, GEMINI-DEBUG prints) isolated the SMP hang to a
# single cluster boundary. With no maxcpus limit, CPU0-7 (both Cortex-A53
# clusters) bring up cleanly in ~35ms; the hang is specifically at CPU8, the
# first core of the third cluster (2x Cortex-A72). Its PSCI CPU_ON SMC never
# returns -- ATF itself hangs -- consistent with B-13 (upstream mtk-scpsys.c
# MT6797 domain-table bug breaking the shared power domains): the A72
# cluster's power domain almost certainly never gets enabled, so CPU_ON
# blocks waiting on a power rail that never comes up. maxcpus=8 boots all 8
# A53 cores (both little clusters) and avoids the A72 cluster entirely --
# an 8x improvement over maxcpus=1 that doesn't require fixing B-13 first.
# Validated logs/2026-07-06-77-maxcpus8/ + logs/2026-07-06-78-maxcpus8-boot.log:
# "smp: Brought up 1 node, 8 CPUs", clean boot to systemd "running" over
# SSH-over-USB. See boot.md "PSCI CPU1 diagnostic" and "maxcpus=8" entries.
# Full SMP (cpu8/9, A72) blocked on the same fix as Phase 5 display (B-13).
# ADDED 2026-07-06 (initcall_debug, TEMPORARY -- remove with B-13 debug
# patches): build #88 proved the whole synchronous display path completes
# (dsi probe tail clean, one benign CMD_DONE irq, panel registered, master
# bind attempt already deferred before the panel print), yet cpu0 still
# wedges silently ~50ms later with IRQs masked and no marker. initcall_debug
# prints "calling <fn>" / "initcall <fn> returned" for every initcall, so
# the last "calling" line in the capture names the exact function that
# wedges, with no further driver instrumentation needed.
# ignore_loglevel added after build #90: initcall_debug prints at
# KERN_DEBUG, which the default console loglevel (7) suppresses -- build
# #90's capture had the flag on the cmdline but zero "calling" lines.
# rcupdate.rcu_cpu_stall_timeout=6 added after build #92: the wedging
# initcall is cacheinfo_sysfs_init -> cpuhp_setup_state(), which waits on
# every online CPU's hotplug thread in turn -- so the real culprit is an
# unresponsive secondary CPU, not cpu0. A 6s RCU stall timeout makes RCU
# name the stuck CPU and attempt a backtrace before the ~12s ATF watchdog
# resets the SoC (default 21s is too late). TEMPORARY, remove with B-13
# debug patches.
# irqchip.gicv3_pseudo_nmi=1 + CONFIG_ARM64_PSEUDO_NMI TRIED AND REVERTED
# (build #96, 2026-07-06): intended to NMI-backtrace cpu0 after the RCU
# stall report named it as the wedged CPU (see build #94/#95 below). Instead
# it broke boot much earlier: capture showed total silence from el3_exit
# (4.37s) straight to the ATF watchdog (14.38s) -- not even the earlycon
# banner printed, worse than the cacheinfo hang this was meant to diagnose.
# Pseudo-NMI's early ICC_PMR_EL1 priority-mask setup (before console init)
# is evidently mishandled by this device's ATF. Do not re-add without a
# different plan for validating ATF/pseudo-NMI compatibility first. See
# boot.md "BUILD #96/#97".
# ADDED 2026-07-07 (cpuidle.off=1 nohlt, TEMPORARY -- B-13 diagnostic):
# build #129 (boot.md "BUILD #129") proved cpu0 survives the entire B-13
# fatal window when hijacked into an irqs-off poll loop -- the core, arch
# counter, UART and GIC MMIO all keep working, so B-13 is not a hardware
# lock. The distinguishing feature of the poll loop vs normal boot is that
# cpu0 never enters idle/WFI. Prime suspect: cpuidle/WFI entry with the MM
# domain/display clocks registered kills interrupt delivery to cpu0 (the
# vendor 3.18 kernel carries its own MT6797 idle-driver hacks).
# cpuidle.off=1 disables the cpuidle framework; nohlt forces poll-idle
# (never executes WFI). If boot completes with these, B-13 is a
# cpuidle/WFI interaction and we have a workaround.
# REMOVED 2026-07-07 same day (build #131, logs/2026-07-07-132-…): NO
# effect — identical hang at 2.63s with both flags confirmed active. So
# the wedge is triggered by taking an interrupt (irqs-off spin survives,
# irqs-on poll-idle dies), not by WFI/idle. See boot.md "BUILD #131".
# REMOVED 2026-07-07: initcall_debug/ignore_loglevel/rcu_cpu_stall_timeout
# were B-13 cpu0-hang diagnostics (see above); B-13 is root-caused and fixed
# (drm/0008), and the flags were flooding the 4MB dmesg ring buffer fast
# enough to evict early boot output (panel/DSI probe messages) within ~2
# minutes of uptime, hiding the data needed for the next debugging pass.
# ADDED 2026-07-08 (g_ether.dev_addr/host_addr): CONFIG_USB_ETH is built-in
# (=y, not a module), so g_ether has no persistent MAC and randomizes one on
# every boot -- macOS keys its Ethernet "service" identity off the MAC, so a
# random MAC means a brand-new enNN interface (and RNDIS/CDC-ECM network
# service) every single boot, indistinguishable in casual troubleshooting
# from "the gadget never comes up", and it defeats any attempt to pin a
# static IP/service on the Mac side. Built-in drivers still honor
# "<modname>.<param>=" on the kernel cmdline, so fix both ends of the link to
# fixed locally-administered MACs (bit 0x02 set, per IEEE 802) instead of
# random ones.
# REMOVED 2026-07-12 (clk_ignore_unused): TEMPORARY flag from the 2026-07-10
# scanout-crash diagnosis, dropped for the production display build (#145).
# The original note:
# clk_ignore_unused re-added for ONE build to separate the two suspects for
# the WDT reset at first real scanout (capture 242): late clk cleanup vs.
# SMI larb0 IOMMU-bypass gap. If the crash persists with this flag, the
# clk path is exonerated.
# ADDED 2026-07-12 (fbcon=rotate:3 + font:TER16x32): first fbcon on the
# physical panel (build #138, banner #122 — vendor video timings). The
# panel is native portrait 1080x2160 but the device is used landscape
# (clamshell keyboard), so rotate the console 90° counter-clockwise (rotate:3); the default
# 8x16 font is unreadably small at ~400 dpi, so use the large Terminus
# 16x32 (CONFIG_FONT_TER16x32=y in gemini-display.config).
# ADDED 2026-07-12 (console=tty0, PERMANENT per user request): render the
# kernel log on the panel (fbcon binds ~1s). Originally a diagnostic for
# the keyboard-build USB-attached hang (serial dies at the B-15 console
# mux switch t=0.45s, so the panel is the only late-boot visibility on
# production/mtu3 builds); kept permanently because on-glass boot logs are
# useful on a device whose serial console is mutually exclusive with USB.
# Listed BEFORE ttyS0 so ttyS0 stays the primary /dev/console.
# ADDED 2026-08-20 (watchdog.open_timeout): close the window in which a
# hung boot cannot recover itself. CONFIG_WATCHDOG_HANDLE_BOOT_ENABLED=y
# means the kernel adopts the RGU watchdog LK left running and pets it from
# a kernel worker -- and with CONFIG_WATCHDOG_OPEN_TIMEOUT=0 it pets it
# FOREVER, waiting for a userspace that may never arrive. Measured
# 2026-08-20: a kernel that booted but never reached userspace sat there for
# 8 minutes, petted by its own kernel, with no reset and no way in. The
# hardware watchdog was armed the whole time and did exactly nothing.
#
# open_timeout gives userspace a deadline to claim /dev/watchdog. systemd
# opens it within a couple of seconds of PID 1 starting (verified:
# "Watchdog running with a hardware timeout of 30s"), so 120 s is a wide
# margin over a normal boot while still turning "userspace never came up"
# into a reset instead of a dead device. This is the single parameter that
# makes an unattended hang recoverable.
#
# NOTE the cmdline here is the ONLY one that takes effect: CONFIG_CMDLINE_FORCE
# discards what LK passes AND what `kexec --command-line` passes. A parameter
# that is not in this string is not set, however it was supplied.
# ADDED then REMOVED 2026-08-23 (nohz=off): kept only long enough to test the
# hypothesis below, which was wrong — the A72 stall was neither WFI nor the
# stopped tick, it was cpu_enable() being run for a late CPU's Spectre caps
# after alternatives had already been patched, which left VBAR_EL1 pointing at
# an unpatched BHB vector table. Original note:
# ADDED 2026-08-23 (nohz=off): the A72s (cluster 2) can now be hotplugged
# online and run at full speed, but each one dies about ten seconds later,
# always in the idle path, recording zero hardirqs and zero cputime while
# remaining powered (MP2_CPU0_PWR_CON keeps PWR_ON), clocked (abist source 37
# = 749988 kHz) and with a GIC CPU interface and redistributor that read back
# byte-identical to a working A53's. It is NOT plain WFI: an arch_cpu_idle()
# patch that makes cluster 2 poll instead of idling did not save it, while a
# pinned busy kthread -- which keeps the core out of the *idle task* rather
# than merely out of WFI -- kept it alive for eight minutes. The remaining
# difference is the tick: NO_HZ stops it on idle entry and relies on a timer
# to restart it. nohz=off keeps a periodic tick on every CPU, which is the
# cheapest test of that hypothesis and costs the A53s far less than forcing
# them all to poll. Remove once the A72 wake path is understood.
# TRIED 2026-08-27 (slub_debug=FZPU slab_nomerge) -- REMOVED, see the verdict
# at the end of this block. Re-add the two words to CONFIG_CMDLINE to run it again.
# The original note:
# name the thing that corrupts memory, instead of the thing that trips over it.
#
# netconsole armed at every boot finally caught #72 from the inside: nine
# wild-pointer faults, all 000182027e9xx109, SEVEN of them 3.1 s after a CONSYS
# func-off. The decisive one was a boot that ran `sync` for eleven minutes with
# no fault, took one hand-run func-off, and faulted on the very next sync. So
# the func-off leaves corruption behind and the next page-cache walk dies on
# it -- which is why drop_caches, remount,ro and sync were each blamed in turn.
#
# Every one of those is the SYMPTOM. The faulting backtrace would only name the
# walker anyway, and it never survives: netconsole truncates ~7 lines into an
# oops, consistently just before the Call trace, and pstore cannot help because
# this SoC's reset does not preserve DRAM (ramoops IS registered and
# /sys/fs/pstore is empty after every crash).
#
# CORRECTED 2026-08-27, build #96 — THE SENTENCE ABOVE IS WRONG, BOTH HALVES.
# It is left in place because it was load-bearing for several days of work and
# deleting it would hide why that work went the way it did.
#
#   * This SoC DOES preserve DRAM across a WDT_SWRST. Measured: magics planted
#     at thirteen reserved physical addresses came back byte-exact after a full
#     reset, five of them from inside ramoops@44410000 itself. The negative
#     control behaved too — lk@46000000 came back holding ARM code, because LK
#     really is reloaded there. scripts/gemini-dram-persistence.sh.
#   * /sys/fs/pstore is empty because systemd-pstore.service EMPTIES IT. It ships
#     enabled on Debian, runs every boot, and moves every record into
#     /var/lib/systemd/pstore/. Eighteen dmesg-ramoops records were sitting there
#     the whole time, back to 2026-08-21, with full register dumps and complete
#     Call traces. End-to-end control: a string written to /dev/pmsg0 came back
#     out of /var/lib/systemd/pstore/pmsg-ramoops-0 after a reboot.
#
# So an oops on this device IS capturable, and always was. Look in
# /var/lib/systemd/pstore/ — `gemini-oops` on the device prints it.
#
# What the archive also shows is that the 000182027e9xx109 faults specifically
# left NO record, while other oopses on the same machine left complete ones.
# That is not a capture failure: the same faults truncate at the same point on
# netconsole, so the kernel itself stops printing a few lines into the report
# and never reaches kmsg_dump(). Two independent channels agreeing on the
# truncation point is evidence about the FAULT, not about the channels.
#
# slub_debug reports at the moment corruption is DETECTED, while the machine is
# still healthy -- so unlike an oops it reaches the journal intact.
#   F = sanity checks       Z = red zoning (catches overruns either side)
#   P = poisoning (catches use-after-free)  U = store alloc/free stack traces
# U is the one that matters: it turns "this object is corrupt" into "allocated
# here, freed there".
#
# slab_nomerge because CONFIG_SLAB_MERGE_DEFAULT=y is set: merged caches make
# attribution ambiguous exactly when it counts.
#
# COST: extra memory per object and a real speed penalty. REMOVE THIS once the
# corruptor is named -- it is a diagnostic, not a setting.
# ADDED 2026-08-27 (netconsole=, #72 diagnostic): arm the kernel log BEFORE
# userspace, because the boots that need watching die before userspace exists.
#
# gemini-netconsole.service arms netconsole from userspace and does it fast
# (399 ms after gemini-usb-net). It still missed every dying boot, and the
# reason is that g_ether is BUILT IN: the host sees "RNDIS/Ethernet Gadget"
# from kernel init whether or not userspace ever runs, so a boot can stall
# arbitrarily early and still enumerate. A dead boot captured on 2026-08-27
# emitted NOTHING -- not even the ~11 s systemd-journald line every healthy
# boot prints -- while the gadget sat enumerated on the host for 120 s.
#
# 120 s is not a coincidence and it is NOT `SETTLE` in this case. There are two
# different 120 s windows on this device and conflating them has now cost two
# separate diagnoses:
#   * SETTLE=120 in gemini-net-up, before the CONSYS func-on
#   * watchdog.open_timeout=120 above -- the kernel pets the watchdog LK left
#     running and gives userspace 120 s to claim /dev/watchdog. A boot that
#     stalls before systemd opens it gets exactly 120 s and is then reset.
# A boot that stalls EARLY dies at 120 s by open_timeout; one that stalls after
# systemd has the watchdog dies at ~31 s by the runtime watchdog. Both
# signatures have now been observed.
#
# netpoll takes the source IP from this string, so it does not need userspace to
# have configured usb0 first -- which is the whole point. The MAC is the
# gadget's host-side address, fixed by g_ether.host_addr above; netconsole does
# not ARP.
#
# NOT TEMPORARY ANY MORE, AND READ THIS BEFORE REMOVING IT.
#
# This went in as a diagnostic and turned out to stop the boot loop. Measured
# on build #94: 14 consecutive reboot cycles, ZERO loops, every boot back in
# ~61 s with Wi-Fi up unaided, plus a 10-minute load soak with no oops and no
# GPU hang. The immediately preceding kernel (#93) differed ONLY by this line
# and looped on roughly half of all reboots -- the measured baseline all night,
# across eight stress runs, was 2 to 6 Preloader events per cycle. 14 clean in
# a row against a ~50% loop rate is p ~ 6e-5, so this is not luck.
#
# I DO NOT KNOW WHY IT WORKS, and that is the important part. Nothing here
# fixes a wild kernel write. The likeliest explanation is timing: netconsole
# from kernel init pushes every printk out over UDP, which slows early boot and
# perturbs whatever race the loop depends on. That makes this a MASK, not a
# cure -- it can regress the moment something else shifts the timing back.
#
# So: keep it, do not treat #72 as closed, and if the loop ever returns this is
# the first thing to suspect rather than the last. The corruption it is hiding
# is still real and still unattributed -- see STATE-2026-08-26-evening.md.
#
# 2026-08-28, #85: RE-TESTED, STILL LOAD-BEARING, AND NOW WE KNOW WHAT IT HIDES.
#
# Build #111 was #110 with this parameter deleted and nothing else changed --
# same tree, same ramdisk, same gemini-reboot. #110 gave 24/24 clean reboot
# cycles; #111 looped on its first reboot, 8 Preloader events over 12 minutes.
# So the paragraph above is right, and DO NOT DELETE THIS PARAMETER.
#
# Two corrections to it, though.
#
# It is not the printk slowdown. netpoll_setup()'s carrier wait -- the only real
# delay this adds -- measures 1-131 ms over 71 captured boots and never comes
# near its 4 s carrier_timeout, because the gadget's carrier is already up.
# ~130 ms is apparently enough, which says the race is extremely tight.
#
# And what it perturbs is not a wild write. A failing boot was captured from the
# inside for the first time, via the ramoops CONSOLE zone (CONFIG_PSTORE_CONSOLE
# is on, the zone is 256 KB, and this SoC preserves DRAM across a reset -- so a
# dead boot is readable on the next live one WITHOUT netconsole):
#
#   [    1.014792] clk: Disabling unused clocks
#   [   24.024316] watchdog: Watchdog detected hard LOCKUP on cpu 2
#   [   24.032318] watchdog: Watchdog detected hard LOCKUP on cpu 7
#   [   36.553169]     in-flight: 77:mmc_rescan
#   [   36.555034]     pending: 13*genpd_power_off_work_fn
#
# Two CPUs hard-lock ~24 s in, mmc_rescan never returns, userspace never opens
# /dev/watchdog, and watchdog.open_timeout=120 above reclaims the machine --
# measured at 119 s from gadget-up to reset. That is the loop.
#
# The suspicion is a power domain being runtime-suspended under something that
# still touches it, and the reason netconsole would perturb it is that
# netpoll_setup() calls dev_open() on usb0 at late_initcall, holding the gadget
# netdev open from kernel init rather than from userspace seconds later.
# Hypothesis, not result. Evidence in 04-docs/captures/85-boot-loop-20260828/.
# TEMPORARILY REMOVED 2026-08-28 (netconsole=, #85 diagnostic build #119):
# netconsole is the mask that hides the clk_disable_unused wedge, so a build
# meant to REPRODUCE that wedge cannot carry it. #111 (this parameter deleted
# and nothing else) looped on its first reboot; #110 with it gave 24/24 clean.
# The trace instrument in drivers/clk/clk.c needs no console at all -- it
# writes into ramconsole@44400000 and prints the previous boot's record on the
# next boot that gets through. PUT THIS BACK the moment the clock is named.
CONFIG_CMDLINE="console=tty0 console=ttyS0,921600n1 earlycon maxcpus=8 nokaslr fbcon=rotate:3 fbcon=font:TER16x32 g_ether.dev_addr=42:00:15:19:82:01 g_ether.host_addr=42:00:15:19:82:00 watchdog.open_timeout=120"
#
# VERDICT (build #92, flashed and run): it caught NOTHING, and that is itself a
# result. Eight full CONSYS func-off/func-on cycles with red-zoning, poisoning
# and stack tracking all active produced zero slab reports and zero oopses. With
# Z on, a plain slab overrun would have been reported -- so the corruption is
# very probably NOT a slab API misuse, which is what slub_debug can see. A DMA
# write into freed or unmapped memory would be invisible to it and fits the
# evidence.
#
# It did change the SIGNATURE usefully, though. Under #92 the wild-pointer
# faults stopped being garbage (000182027e9xx109) and became a single stable
# kernel address, twice:
#
#   Unable to handle kernel paging request at ffff800081e470d0
#   ESR = 0x0000000096000046      <- a WRITE, level 2 translation fault
#
# and with nokaslr that resolves: it lies between __rela_start (ffff800081dca430)
# and __rela_end (ffff80008250af08) -- inside the kernel image's READ-ONLY
# relocation data. Something is writing into kernel rodata, during Wi-Fi
# operation, a few tens of seconds after a CONSYS func-ON.
#
# REMOVED from the daily kernel because it costs real memory and speed on a
# device that is somebody's daily driver, and it had stopped paying for itself.
# The next attempt should chase the writer, not the slab: the address above is
# fixed and the kernel is nokaslr, so a hardware watchpoint on it is the obvious
# instrument.
CONFIG_CMDLINE_FORCE=y

# REVERTED 2026-07-04: previously disabled CONFIG_RANDOMIZE_BASE/RELOCATABLE
# here, chasing a text_offset=0x0 red herring. That was wrong and actively
# harmful: RELOCATABLE=y (the arch/arm64 default) is the exact mechanism that
# lets a legacy/naive bootloader like this device's LK boot a modern kernel
# at all. LK hardcodes the kernel load address to dram_base+0x80000
# (0x40080000) -- confirmed 2026-07-04 by overriding boot.img's kernel_addr
# field to a 2MB-aligned address and observing LK still print
# "[LK]jump to K64 0x40080000" (the override was silently ignored). 0x40080000
# is NOT 2MB-aligned, so the kernel depends on its own early relocation code
# (gated by CONFIG_RELOCATABLE) to copy itself to a valid boundary before
# continuing. Disabling RELOCATABLE removed that self-relocation and is the
# likely reason every post-el3_exit capture has been silent. Leaving both
# configs at their defconfig defaults (unset here = default y) going forward.
# See boot.md "Aligned kernel_addr retested -- LK ignores header field"
# (2026-07-04).
